<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/gotop.owl#"
     xml:base="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/gotop.owl"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:gotop="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/gotop.owl#"
     xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
     xmlns:cpannotationschema="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/schemas/cpannotationschema.owl#">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/gotop.owl">
        <owl:imports rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/schemas/cpannotationschema.owl"/>
        <dcterms:description rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To represent types of gene-related entities and their parts.</dcterms:description>
        <dcterms:title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GO Top Pattern</dcterms:title>
        <cpannotationschema:coversRequirements>What are the possible most general supertypes for any type from the gene-related domain?</cpannotationschema:coversRequirements>
        <cpannotationschema:coversRequirements>What entity is part of another entity in the gene-related domain?</cpannotationschema:coversRequirements>
        <cpannotationschema:extractedFrom>http://purl.org/obo/owl/gene_ontology_edit</cpannotationschema:extractedFrom>
        <cpannotationschema:hasConsequences>It allows to categorize gene-related types into three main types.
It allows to represent parts of gene-related entities (transitively).
It does not allow to represent the relationships between entities of different types.
It does not say anything about disjointness of categories.</cpannotationschema:hasConsequences>
        <cpannotationschema:hasIntent rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To represent types of gene-related entities and their parts.</cpannotationschema:hasIntent>
        <cpannotationschema:reengineeredFrom>http://purl.org/obo/owl/gene_ontology_edit</cpannotationschema:reengineeredFrom>
        <cpannotationschema:relatedCPs>http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/partof.owl can have this pattern as a specialization, if the three gene-related categories are considered as owl:subClassOf http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/cp/owl/partof.owl#Entity.</cpannotationschema:relatedCPs>
        <cpannotationschema:scenarios>A &apos;template for synthesis of G-rich strand of telomere DNA activity&apos; must be part of a &apos;telomerase activity&apos;.</cpannotationschema:scenarios>
        <cpannotationschema:scenarios>All &apos;templates for synthesis of G-rich strand of telomere DNA activity&apos; are &apos;molecular functions&apos;.</cpannotationschema:scenarios>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Curated by OntoBoard 1.155.0, so that the pattern passes ODK&apos;s quality report with its default profile, names every term with a label, and is consistent and coherent. Changes: label added x11; term declared x2;
- header: dcterms:title &quot;GO Top Pattern&quot; - the library&apos;s name for the pattern
- header: dcterms:description - the library&apos;s description of the pattern
Each change is listed under &quot;curated&quot; in the pattern&apos;s metadata.json in OntoBoard&apos;s pattern library.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>This pattern is peculiar to the biomolecular domain, where types of entities, rather than specific instances, are the main focus of interest. As a result, the Gene Ontology, based on this top-level categories, is a huge TBox.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">0.1</owl:versionInfo>
        <owl:versionInfo rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">1.1 added rdfs:isDefinedBy for all named entities</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


